@meta
id: 21-02
title: The last macro, again
chapter: 21
type: lesson
skills: macro-play
par: 52
minutes: 5
vscode: ten cursors, Home, type, End, type
open: customers.fixture.ts

@brief
The seed script needs customer records and someone pasted the email
column straight out of the spreadsheet. Every address in the array
becomes an object:

    { email: 'alice.anderson@example.com' },

Ten lines. Record the edit on the first, then play it on the rest.

The one new thing: `@a` is two keys and you are about to press it nine
times. Vim remembers which macro you last played, and `@@` plays that
one again. It is to `@a` what `;` is to `f`.

@keys
| `qa` … `q` | record into `a` |
| `@a` | play `a` |
| `@@` | play whichever macro you played last |

@hints
The edit is an insert at the front of the line and an append at the
end, with `Esc` between them. Both are in the recording.
--
`^i{ email: '` then `Esc`, then `A' },` and `Esc`, then `j`.
--
`j`, record with `qa^i{ email: '<Esc>A' },<Esc>jq`, then `@a` once
and `@@` eight times.

@solution
    j  qa ^i{ email: ' <Esc> A' }, <Esc> j q   @a   @@ × 8   :wq<Enter>

Thirty-six keystrokes for ten lines.

`@@` is a small thing and you will use it constantly, because the
rhythm of macro work is: record, play once, look, and if it did what
you wanted, play it again and again. `@@` is the "again". It also
survives switching files, so a macro recorded in one buffer can be
replayed in another with the same two keys.

The next lesson replaces the eight presses with one count. But `@@` is
still the right tool when you want to watch each replay land before
committing to the next, which is most of the time on a file you have
not read carefully.

@start customers.fixture.ts
export const customers = [
  alice.anderson@example.com
  ben.okafor@example.com
  chloe.martin@example.com
  dev.patel@example.com
